Start the robot
Terminal 1:

bash
cd ~/simple_gazebo_robot
gz sim simple_robot_world.sdf
Make sure the play button in Gazebo is active. If the simulation is paused, the robot will not move.

Drive it from Terminal 2
Move forward
bash
gz topic -t /cmd_vel \
  -m gz.msgs.Twist \
  -p 'linear: {x: 0.5}, angular: {z: 0.0}'
The robot should move forward.

Move backward
bash
gz topic -t /cmd_vel \
  -m gz.msgs.Twist \
  -p 'linear: {x: -0.5}, angular: {z: 0.0}'
Turn left
bash
gz topic -t /cmd_vel \
  -m gz.msgs.Twist \
  -p 'linear: {x: 0.0}, angular: {z: 0.8}'
Turn right
bash
gz topic -t /cmd_vel \
  -m gz.msgs.Twist \
  -p 'linear: {x: 0.0}, angular: {z: -0.8}'
Move in a curve
bash
gz topic -t /cmd_vel \
  -m gz.msgs.Twist \
  -p 'linear: {x: 0.4}, angular: {z: 0.4}'
Important limitation
Each command above is sent once, so the robot may only move briefly or not visibly enough depending on the plugin timeout behavior. For continuous movement, run this command repeatedly:

bash
while true; do
  gz topic -t /cmd_vel \
    -m gz.msgs.Twist \
    -p 'linear: {x: 0.5}, angular: {z: 0.0}'
  sleep 0.1
done
Stop it using:

text
Ctrl + C
Then send a zero-speed command:

bash
gz topic -t /cmd_vel \
  -m gz.msgs.Twist \
  -p 'linear: {x: 0.0}, angular: {z: 0.0}'
Verify the command topic
Before driving, confirm that the topic exists:

bash
gz topic -l | grep cmd_vel
You should see:

text
/cmd_vel
You can also check the robot’s odometry:

bash
gz topic -e -t /odom
